  },
  {
    "id": "4782682722714304636",
    "title": "Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization",
    "url": "https://dustanddynasty.blogspot.com/2011/08/heritage-harappa-pakistan-indus-valley-ancient-city-unesco-world-heritage.html",
    "updated": "2026-10-29T23:45:28+05:00",
    "content": "\n<script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"BlogPosting\", \"headline\": \"Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\", \"description\": \"Harappa — The Lost City One of the Greatest Urban Centers of the Ancient World, and the Heart of the Indus Valley Civilization 📍 &nbsp; Location: &nbsp;Sahiwal \", \"datePublished\": \"2011-08-13T14:57:00+05:00\", \"dateModified\": \"2026-10-11T16:20:51+05:00\", \"author\": {\"@type\": \"Person\", \"name\": \"Nasir A Naqvi\", \"url\": \"https://dustanddynasty.blogspot.com/p/about-me-and-journey.html\"}, \"publisher\": {\"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"Dust and Dynasty\", \"url\": \"https://dustanddynasty.blogspot.com\"}, \"mainEntityOfPage\": {\"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"@id\": \"https://dustanddynasty.blogspot.com/2011/08/heritage-harappa-pakistan-indus-valley-ancient-city-unesco-world-heritage.html\"}, \"inLanguage\": \"en\", \"image\": \"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhXiCEnYf-gyjOP9TGy2hSQFxzYgdDLiO0lI5MJlIHGZdFRaDSX61PqUDpkjs-trYDtUguNLcpnGRHHt7CGms7XJw2we_5TX-VFcZqTv5Pkx_PafyJo9M0smwsFKL5t7qsJXrVWtAuT9IvsnS-q1fQRK5kgELItx0xVumpPbw4v0wA4XVY1GUDynFYr5iMq/w640-h360/Harrapa_Mound_Aryan_Civilisation_birth_place.jpg\", \"keywords\": \"Asia, Pakistan\"}\n</script>\n<div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\" trbidi=\"on\"><h2 style=\"text-align: left;\"><span style=\"font-size: large;\">Harappa — The Lost City</span></h2>\n<h3 style=\"text-align: left;\"><i><span face=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">One of the Greatest Urban Centers of the Ancient\nWorld, and the Heart of the Indus Valley Civilization</span></span></i></h3>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>📍</span> <b>Location:</b> Sahiwal\nDistrict, Punjab, Pakistan<br/>\n<span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>📅</span> <b>Period:</b> c.\n3300–1300 B.C. (Mature Harappan: c. 2600–1900 B.C.)<br/>\n<span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>🏷️</span> <b>Category:</b> Archaeological\n/ Civilizational<br/>\n<span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>⚠️</span> <b>Status:</b> UNESCO\nWorld Heritage Tentative List; Federal Protected Site<br/>\n<span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>🔢</span> <b>Classification:</b> Indus\nValley Civilization — Type Site<br/>\n<span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>📏</span> <b>Significance:</b> Archaeological,\nHistorical, Civilizational<br/>\n<span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>🗺️</span> <b>Coordinates:</b> 30°37′N\n72°52′E<o:p></o:p></p><!--more--><figure style=\"margin: 24px 0px; text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Inauguration_Plate_of_Link_Road_to_Harappa_Museum_and_Site.JPG/1280px-Inauguration_Plate_of_Link_Road_to_Harappa_Museum_and_Site.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" style=\"border-radius: 6px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;\"/>\n<figcaption style=\"color: #666666; font-size: 0.92em; font-style: italic; margin-top: 8px;\">A view of Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>The Name That Named a Civilization<o:p></o:p></b></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa gave its name to a civilization. This is worth\npausing over. Not every archaeological site achieves such distinction. Troy did\nit. Mycenae did it. And Harappa did it — a mound of brick and earth on the flat\nalluvial plain of the Punjab, beside the old course of the Ravi River, in what\nis now Sahiwal District.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The civilization it names — the Harappan, or Indus Valley\nCivilization — was one of the three great urban cultures of the ancient world,\ncontemporary with Mesopotamia and Egypt. At its height, between approximately\n2600 and 1900 B.C., it extended across an area larger than either of its\ncontemporaries, stretching from the foothills of the Himalayas to the Arabian\nSea coast, from the Makran in the west to the upper Ganges-Yamuna Doab in the\neast. Its cities were planned. Its drains were engineered. Its script,\ninscribed on thousands of small steatite seals, remains undeciphered.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa was one of the civilization's largest cities. It was\nalso the first to be identified by modern archaeology — and the first to be\ndamaged by it.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: center;\" trbidi=\"on\"><i><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\">Clay toys of Harrapa</span></i></div><div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"font-weight: bold; text-align: left;\" trbidi=\"on\"><b><br/></b></div><b>Discovery and Destruction</b>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Charles Masson, a deserter from the East India Company's\narmy who reinvented himself as an antiquarian traveller, noted the ruins of\nHarappa in the 1820s. Alexander Cunningham, during his surveys for the\nArchaeological Survey of India, visited the site in the 1850s and 1870s,\nrecording brick structures and collecting small artefacts, including the first\nHarappan seals to be documented.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">But it was the railway that did the damage. In the 1850s,\nengineers constructing the Lahore-Multan railway line used bricks quarried from\nthe ancient mounds of Harappa as ballast for the track bed. Millions of bricks\n— fired four thousand years ago, laid with precision by the builders of one of\nhumanity's earliest cities — were torn from their original positions and\ncrushed beneath railway sleepers. The upper strata of the site were destroyed\nbefore systematic archaeology could record them.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">This act of industrial vandalism was not malicious. It was\nignorant. The significance of the site was not yet understood. But the damage\nwas irreversible, and it haunts the archaeology of Harappa to this day.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-family: inherit;\"><i>Harrapan terracotta figurine</i></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>The Excavated City</b></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Systematic excavation at Harappa began in the 1920s under\nRai Bahadur Daya Ram Sahni, working under the direction of Sir John Marshall,\nthen Director-General of the Archaeological Survey of India. These excavations,\ntogether with the contemporaneous work at Mohenjo-daro under R.D. Banerji and\nlater Marshall, revealed the existence of the Indus Valley Civilization for the\nfirst time.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Subsequent excavations — by Mortimer Wheeler in the 1940s,\nby Pakistani archaeologists in the decades following independence, and by the\nHarappa Archaeological Research Project (HARP) led by George Dales and later\nRichard Meadow and Jonathan Mark Kenoyer from the 1980s onward — have greatly\nrefined our understanding of the site.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa covered an area of approximately 150 hectares at its\nmaximum extent. The city was organized into several distinct mounded areas:<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Mound AB</b> — the so-called \"citadel,\"\ncontaining large-scale architecture including platforms and what may have been\npublic or ceremonial buildings.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Mound E and ET</b> — areas of dense residential and\ncraft-production activity.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Mound F</b> — containing the famous\n\"granary\" (a designation now disputed; the structures may have served\nother storage or industrial functions).<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Cemetery H</b> — a late-period burial ground whose\ndistinctive painted pottery defines the terminal phase of occupation at the\nsite.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The city possessed a sophisticated system of drains, wells,\nand water management. Streets were laid out in a broadly grid-like pattern.\nHouses were constructed of standardized fired brick, with dimensions conforming\nto a consistent ratio. The standardization is remarkable. It implies central\nplanning, or at least a powerful shared convention.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>The Seals and the Script</b></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The steatite seals of Harappa are among the most distinctive\nartefacts of the ancient world. Small, square, and finely carved, they\ntypically depict an animal — most commonly the so-called \"unicorn,\" a\nbull-like creature shown in profile — accompanied by a short inscription in the\nIndus script.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The script remains undeciphered. Hundreds of attempts have\nbeen made. None has achieved scholarly consensus. The inscriptions are short —\nthe average is fewer than five signs — which limits the statistical methods\navailable for analysis. The language underlying the script is unknown: proposed\ncandidates include Dravidian, early Indo-Aryan, and various language isolates.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The seals themselves appear to have served an administrative\nfunction — as markers of identity, ownership, or authority, used to stamp\nimpressions into wet clay on the closures of packages and containers. They are\nevidence of a bureaucratic or commercial system of some complexity.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>What Harappa Does Not Tell Us</b></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa is eloquent in its silences. Unlike Mesopotamia, it\nhas yielded no readable texts, no king-lists, no legal codes, no literary\ncompositions. Unlike Egypt, it offers no monumental tombs, no temples of\nrecognizable form, no representations of rulers. The political structure of\nHarappan society remains unknown. Were there kings? Councils? Priests?\nMerchants who governed by consensus? We do not know.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The absence of obvious palatial architecture, of monumental\nsculpture, and of military iconography has led some scholars to hypothesize a\nsociety governed not by despotic rulers but by some form of collective or\ntheocratic authority. This remains speculation. The material evidence is\ncompatible with multiple interpretations.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">What Harappa does tell us — with the mute clarity of its\nbricks, its drains, its weights, and its seals — is that a large,\nwell-organized urban population lived here for centuries, conducted\nlong-distance trade, maintained consistent standards of measurement and\nconstruction, and then, around 1900 B.C., began to decline. By 1300 B.C., the\nsite was largely abandoned.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>The Site Today</b></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa is a protected site managed by the Department of\nArchaeology and Museums, Government of Pakistan. A site museum, established in\n1967 and updated in subsequent decades, displays artefacts recovered from the\nexcavations.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The condition of the site is mixed. The areas excavated by\nHARP have been documented to a high standard, and some conservation measures\nhave been implemented. But the site faces ongoing challenges: encroachment from\nthe adjacent modern town of Harappa, agricultural activity on the periphery,\nand the general difficulty of maintaining exposed mud-brick and fired-brick\narchitecture in a climate of extreme summer heat and monsoon rainfall.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa has been on Pakistan's UNESCO World Heritage\nTentative List. Its formal nomination has been complicated by site management\nissues and the competing priorities of other heritage sites. The irony is\nconsiderable: the site that gave its name to one of the world's great\ncivilizations does not yet enjoy the formal international recognition that such\nstatus would seem to warrant.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>The Weight of a Name</b></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">To stand at Harappa is to stand at the origin point of a\nname that appears in every world history textbook written in the last century.\nIt is to stand where the idea of urban civilization in South Asia was first\ndiscovered — and first destroyed. The railway bricks are gone. The upper strata\nare gone. Much that might have been known will never be known.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">But the lower strata remain. The seals remain. The questions\nremain. And the name remains — Harappa — attached now not merely to a mound in\nthe Punjab but to an entire civilization, a civilization that built its cities\nwithout kings we can name, wrote its texts in a script we cannot read, and\nvanished for reasons we do not understand.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Some mysteries are richer than solutions. Harappa is one of\nthem.<o:p></o:p></p><h3>What Harappa's Material Remains Tell Us About Daily Life</h3><p>The importance of Harappa extends beyond its monumental ruins. Archaeological objects recovered from the settlement provide evidence about manufacturing, food production, personal ornamentation, exchange and burial practices. Pottery, beads, tools, seals and other artefacts allow researchers to investigate how people used materials and organized different aspects of their lives. However, an artefact does not always reveal its original purpose by itself. Its archaeological context, the layer in which it was discovered and its relationship to other finds are essential for developing a reliable interpretation.</p><p>Harappan seals and standardized weights are particularly valuable for investigating economic relationships and systems of measurement. Seals bearing distinctive symbols and animal motifs are found at multiple sites associated with the Indus Civilization. Their distribution supports research into communication, administration and exchange, although the precise meaning of many symbols remains uncertain because the Indus script has not been conclusively deciphered. It would therefore be inaccurate to claim that every seal had a known commercial or religious purpose. The evidence instead demonstrates that the civilization developed sophisticated material practices whose full social and political significance remains an active subject of research.</p><h3>Harappa and the Limits of Archaeological Interpretation</h3><p>Harappa also illustrates an important principle of historical research: the difference between physical evidence and explanations proposed to interpret it. Archaeologists can examine building remains, artefacts, burial contexts and changes between occupation layers. These observations can then be used to develop hypotheses about social organization, economic activity and cultural change. Nevertheless, the absence of deciphered written records limits what can be stated confidently about individual rulers, political institutions and the precise beliefs of Harappan communities.</p><p>A careful account of Harappa should therefore distinguish established findings from unresolved questions. This does not diminish the achievements of the Indus Civilization. Instead, it shows why archaeological research remains important: new excavations, scientific methods and comparisons between sites can refine earlier interpretations. By examining both what is known and what remains uncertain, readers gain a more accurate understanding of Harappa as an archaeological site and as part of a much wider Bronze Age civilization.</p>\n<h1 style=\"margin-bottom: 6.0pt; margin-left: 0in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 30.0pt; margin: 30pt 0in 6pt;\"><b><span style=\"color: #2c1810; font-size: 14pt;\">Harappa — The Lost City\nof the Indus Valley</span></b></h1><h2 style=\"margin: 10pt 0in 6pt; text-align: left;\"><b><span style=\"color: #8b6914; font-size: 11pt;\">✦\nConclusion</span></b></h2><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 8pt;\">Harappa is where the story of\nSouth Asian civilisation truly begins. Long before the Vedas were composed,\nlong before the first Greek philosopher asked his first question, the people of\nHarappa were building planned cities with drainage systems, granaries, and\nstandardised weights and measures that would not be seen again in this part of\nthe world for thousands of years. They were, in the truest sense, ahead of\ntheir time.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 8pt;\">Discovered in 1921 by Rai\nBahadur Daya Ram Sahni, Harappa gave its name to an entire civilisation — the\nHarappan or Indus Valley Civilisation — that flourished between approximately\n2600 and 1900 BCE across a territory larger than Egypt and Mesopotamia combined.\nThe scale of what these people achieved, and the mystery of why their\ncivilisation declined, has fascinated archaeologists and historians for over a\ncentury.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 8pt;\">Visiting Harappa today is a\nhumbling experience. The mounds that represent what was once a city of perhaps\n50,000 people have been partially excavated, but much remains buried. Every\nseason of excavation reveals new details about a society that was sophisticated,\nurban, and literate in a writing system we still cannot fully read. Harappa is\nnot just Pakistan's ancient past — it is humanity's ancient past, and one of\nthe great unresolved puzzles of world history.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 6.0pt; margin-left: 0in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 14.0pt; margin: 14pt 0in 6pt;\"><b><span style=\"color: #8b6914; font-size: 11pt;\">✦\nFrequently Asked Questions</span></b><o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 3.0pt; margin-left: 0in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 9.0pt; margin: 9pt 0in 3pt;\"><b><span style=\"color: #2c1810;\">Q1: When was Harappa\ndiscovered?</span></b><o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 5.0pt; margin-left: .25in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 0in; margin: 0in 0in 5pt 0.25in;\">Harappa was first systematically excavated in 1921 by Rai\nBahadur Daya Ram Sahni of the Archaeological Survey of India, though local\nknowledge of the site and opportunistic removal of its bricks for railway\nconstruction had been occurring for decades prior.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 3.0pt; margin-left: 0in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 9.0pt; margin: 9pt 0in 3pt;\"><b><span style=\"color: #2c1810;\">Q2: How large was the ancient\ncity of Harappa?</span></b><o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 5.0pt; margin-left: .25in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 0in; margin: 0in 0in 5pt 0.25in;\">At its peak around 2000 BCE, Harappa is estimated to have\nhad a population of 23,500 to 50,000 people, making it one of the largest\ncities in the ancient world, with a sophisticated urban layout covering several\nsquare kilometres.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 3.0pt; margin-left: 0in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 9.0pt; margin: 9pt 0in 3pt;\"><b><span style=\"color: #2c1810;\">Q3: Why hasn't the Indus script\nbeen deciphered yet?</span></b><o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 5.0pt; margin-left: .25in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 0in; margin: 0in 0in 5pt 0.25in;\">The Indus script, found on thousands of seals and tablets\nfrom Harappa and other sites, remains undeciphered despite decades of scholarly\neffort. The absence of a bilingual text (like the Rosetta Stone) and the short\naverage length of inscriptions make decipherment extremely challenging.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 3.0pt; margin-left: 0in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 9.0pt; margin: 9pt 0in 3pt;\"><b><span style=\"color: #2c1810;\">Q4: Is Harappa a UNESCO World\nHeritage Site?</span></b><o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom: 5.0pt; margin-left: .25in; margin-right: 0in; margin-top: 0in; margin: 0in 0in 5pt 0.25in;\">Harappa is not yet individually inscribed as a UNESCO World\nHeritage Site, though there have been sustained advocacy efforts to achieve\nthis. It is protected under Pakistani law as a site of national heritage\nsignificance.<o:p></o:p></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>📊</span> Summary Table of\nHistorical Facts</b></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: none; border-style: none; border-width: medium; border: none; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .25pt; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; width: 768px;\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<td nowrap=\"\" style=\"border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Detail<o:p></o:p></b></p>\n</td>\n<td nowrap=\"\" style=\"border-left: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Information<o:p></o:p></b></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Site Name</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Location</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Sahiwal District, Punjab, Pakistan<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Civilization</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Indus Valley (Harappan) Civilization<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Date Range</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">c. 3300–1300 B.C. (Mature phase: 2600–1900 B.C.)<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Area</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">~150 hectares<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>First Noted</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Charles Masson, 1820s<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Systematic Excavation</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Daya Ram Sahni (1920s); Mortimer Wheeler (1940s); HARP\n  (1986–present)<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Key Finds</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Steatite seals, standardized bricks, drainage systems,\n  Cemetery H culture<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Script</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Indus script — undeciphered<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Damage</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Upper strata destroyed by 19th-century railway brick\n  quarrying<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Museum</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Harappa Archaeological Museum (est. 1967)<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>UNESCO Status</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Tentative List<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Current Condition</b><o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Partially conserved; subject to encroachment and\n  weathering<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br/></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>🧳</span> Visitor's Guide<o:p></o:p></b></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border-color: currentcolor; border-image: none; border-style: none; border-width: medium; border: none; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .25pt; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; width: 768px;\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<td nowrap=\"\" style=\"border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Detail<o:p></o:p></b></p>\n</td>\n<td nowrap=\"\" style=\"border-left: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b>Information<o:p></o:p></b></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Nearest City<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Sahiwal (~24 km)<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Access<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">By road from Lahore (~200 km); rail via Harappa station<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Best Season<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">October to March<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Current Status<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Accessible; museum operational<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Entry Fee<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Nominal (check current rates)<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-top: none; border: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Advisory<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-color: currentcolor rgb(229, 231, 235) rgb(229, 231, 235) currentcolor; border-left: none; border-right: 1pt solid rgb(229, 231, 235); border-style: none solid solid none; border-top: none; border-width: medium 1pt 1pt medium; mso-border-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid #E5E7EB .25pt; padding: 0.75pt;\" valign=\"bottom\">\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Combine with Lahore heritage circuit; allow half day for\n  site and museum<o:p></o:p></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span face='\"Segoe UI Emoji\",sans-serif' style='mso-bidi-font-family: \"Segoe UI Emoji\";'>📚</span> Sources &amp; Further\nReading<o:p></o:p></b></p>\n<ul style=\"margin-top: 0in;\" type=\"disc\">\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\">Kenoyer,\n     Jonathan Mark. <i>Ancient Cities of the Indus Valley Civilization</i> (1998)<o:p></o:p></li>\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\">Possehl,\n     Gregory L. <i>The Indus Civilization: A Contemporary Perspective</i> (2002)<o:p></o:p></li>\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\">Wheeler,\n     Mortimer. <i>The Indus Civilization</i> (1968)<o:p></o:p></li>\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\">Marshall,\n     John. <i>Mohenjo-daro and the Indus Civilization</i> (1931)<o:p></o:p></li>\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\">Meadow,\n     Richard H. &amp; Kenoyer, J.M. \"Harappa Archaeological Research\n     Project\" — various field reports<o:p></o:p></li>\n<li class=\"MsoNormal\">Cunningham,\n     Alexander. <i>Archaeological Survey of India Reports</i>, Vol. V</li><li class=\"MsoNormal\"><a href=\"https://archaeology.punjab.gov.pk/arch-sites-harappa?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Government of Punjab — Archaeological Sites of Harappa</a></li></ul>\n</div>\n<iframe allowfullscreen=\"\" height=\"450\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer-when-downgrade\" src=\"https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=!1m18!1m12!1m3!1d6866.347974254388!2d72.85723879650516!3d30.62905380000001!2m3!1f0!2f0!3f0!3m2!1i1024!2i768!4f13.1!3m3!1m2!1s0x3922d11d607d108b%3A0xd83a10926ef234e7!2sHarappa%20Old%20City!5e0!3m2!1sen!2s!4v1770400316912!5m2!1sen!2s\" style=\"border: 0;\" width=\"600\"></iframe>\n<figure style=\"margin: 24px 0px; text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Archaeological_Museum%2C_Harappa_exhibit.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled\" style=\"border-radius: 6px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;\"/>\n<figcaption style=\"color: #666666; font-size: 0.92em; font-style: italic; margin-top: 8px;\">A view of Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization.</figcaption>\n</figure><p style=\"font-size: 0.8em; color: gray; text-align: center;\"><i>Image source: Wikimedia Commons</i></p>\n<figure style=\"margin: 24px 0px; text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Archaeological_Museum-Harappa_by_smn121-4.JPG/1280px-Archaeological_Museum-Harappa_by_smn121-4.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" style=\"border-radius: 6px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;\"/>\n<figcaption style=\"color: #666666; font-size: 0.92em; font-style: italic; margin-top: 8px;\">A view of Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure style=\"margin: 24px 0px; text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Harappa_%28asad_aman%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled\" style=\"border-radius: 6px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;\"/>\n<figcaption style=\"color: #666666; font-size: 0.92em; font-style: italic; margin-top: 8px;\">A view of Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization.</figcaption>\n</figure><p style=\"font-size: 0.8em; color: gray; text-align: center;\"><i>Image source: Wikimedia Commons</i></p>\n<figure style=\"margin: 24px 0px; text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Harappa_Archeology_1.jpg/1280px-Harappa_Archeology_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" style=\"border-radius: 6px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;\"/>\n<figcaption style=\"color: #666666; font-size: 0.92em; font-style: italic; margin-top: 8px;\">A view of Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure style=\"margin: 24px 0px; text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Harappa_Archeology_2.jpg/1280px-Harappa_Archeology_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" style=\"border-radius: 6px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;\"/>\n<figcaption style=\"color: #666666; font-size: 0.92em; font-style: italic; margin-top: 8px;\">A view of Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure style=\"margin: 24px 0px; text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Harappa_Archeology_3.jpg/1280px-Harappa_Archeology_3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=imageinfo&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" style=\"border-radius: 6px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;\"/>\n<figcaption style=\"color: #666666; font-size: 0.92em; font-style: italic; margin-top: 8px;\">A view of Harappa — The Lost City | Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<div style=\"background: rgb(253, 246, 227); border-radius: 8px; border: 2px solid rgb(139, 105, 20); margin: 30px 0px; padding: 20px; text-align: center;\">\n<h3 style=\"color: #2c1810; margin-bottom: 5px;\">🌍 Support Dust and Dynasty</h3>\n<p style=\"color: #555555; font-size: 13px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    This encyclopedia is free for everyone. If it brought value to you, \n    please consider supporting the research behind it.\n  </p>\n\n    \n  \n</div><p>\nWhile exploring <strong>Harappa, Pakistan — The Lost City of the Indus Valley Civilization</strong>, you may also enjoy reading \n<a href=\"https://dustanddynasty.blogspot.com/2011/08/heritage-mohenjo-daro-pakistan-indus-valley-civilization-unesco-world-heritage.html\">Moenjodaro, Pakistan — The 5000-Year-Old City That Vanished | UNESCO World Heritage</a>, which expands the historical narrative \nand connects related civilizations and archaeological discoveries.\n</p>\n<br/>\n<!--Start of Dust and Dynasty Sidebar Support Banner-->\n<div class=\"sidebar-support-banner\">\n<h3>🌍 Support Dust and Dynasty</h3>\n<p>Your support funds the research and hosting that keeps this encyclopedia free for everyone.</p>\n</div>\n<style>\n  .sidebar-support-banner {\n    background-color: #FDFBF7; \n    border: 1px solid #E6DBCB; \n    border-radius: 8px;\n    padding: 15px;\n    text-align: center;\n    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;\n    margin-top: 40px;\n    margin-bottom: 20px;\n  }\n  .sidebar-support-banner h3 { color: #2C3E50; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 16px; }\n  .sidebar-support-banner p { color: #5A6C7A; margin-bottom: 15px; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.4; }\n  .sidebar-button-group { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }\n  .sidebar-btn { display: block; padding: 10px 15px; border-radius: 6px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; font-size: 14px; transition: background-color 0.3s ease; text-align: center; }\n  .sidebar-btn-one-time { background-color: #D4A373; color: #FFFFFF; }\n  .sidebar-btn-one-time:hover { background-color: #C39262; }\n  .sidebar-btn-monthly { background-color: #2A5C43; color: #FFFFFF; }\n  .sidebar-btn-monthly:hover { background-color: #1E422F; }\n</style>\n<!--End of Dust and Dynasty Sidebar Support Banner-->\n"
